d  ask their people and the veterans of  their organization to keep the tension  at a minimum feeling that some way a  proper and a dignified and a solution  would be found so that there would be  peace in the world and freest would not  cause war misma Haiti I trust there were  no veterans of the Red Army there but  may I ask you you fellows really  pacifists no we are not pacifists we are  trying to perfect the brotherhood of man  and world peace we are principled  citizens of America who if our  principles of democracy are infringed  upon will fight for our country we will  fight for those things that we believe  will lead to the dignity of mankind and  the Brotherhood of mankind and in that  connection commander what's your  position on Korea for example they get  to a specific case well my convention  decided that if the talks in Korea  failed that they would advocate all-out  war so that no that the principal of  non-aggression that the United Nations  has enunciated would be upheld but not a  return to limited war that we do not  believe in a limited a return to limited  war Oh mr. media I gather that the  veterans you visited were members of the  free world that's right with the  exception of Yugoslavia Yugoslavia had  their delegates there and they were very  cooperative and I believe they are  genuinely entering into this world  convention of veterans in an attempt to  bring a common problem that faces us all  together so that we can enunciate a new  mode of life for the whole world there  are German a Japanese veterans in this  or there is a German unit which was just  introduced at this latest convention we  feel that we cannot exclude any nation  and we must bring all nations in as  rapidly as possible otherwise how can  you have world peace with only half the  world sitting at the counsel table well  commander I gather you learned a few  things to some of these European  soldiers you think they learn anything  from you American veterans I think we  did America's service in this way they  saw that we've just weren't maintaining  our  point of view that we would generously  sit down with them in a friendly way and  discuss their point of view I came away  with the feeling that there was a  tremendous force generated a tremendous  force mobilized for world peace because  I was treated as a friend and a neighbor  by all of these veterans besides having  the mission of keeping the peace in this  organization you do some specific things  like rehabilitation of veterans  themselves do you know yes we do there  are 4 million veterans in the world  outside of America who are disabled and  in need of prosthetic devices for  instance we promised the gentleman from  Israel that we would bring one of his  men who does not have an arm to America  so that he can have the type of  prosthetic device that Harold Russell  where's he's being brought here by my  organization and will be given a new  life with this new prosthetic device  this is a particular contribution you  Americans can make it isn't it since  you're probably ahead in this country on  prosthesis we are the leaders today in  the world of veteran rehabilitation and  in the use of new prosthetic devices for  instance when we were in Holland how  Russell went in to see a veteran who  hadn't been out of his house in over 10  years the men broke into tears when  Harold showed him how he could use his  new prosthetic devices and he agreed to  take training with the help of our world  veterans fund in order that he too would  have a new life just like Harold had  arrived at a new way of life after being  having his hands decapitated welcome and  right I gather that the veterans of  Europe didn't get quite as good deal as  the veterans of United States were they  envious of you chaps well of course you  understand we are very well favoured  economically in this company there's a  very great comparison between our way of  life and the way of life of the people  in European countries but I sense no  sense of jealousy I did since a great  need in the hearts of those people and I  did sense that they wanted our help and  would cooperate with us in every way  possible to achieve world peace  and to help these poor veterans who need  help well incidentally sir I understand  that your am vet organization favors  universal military training you get  anywhere on that well we are making a  big fight for universal military  training and I'll tell you why the  veterans of the last war have been  called back many of them to fight in  korea and we feel that a man who's given  four or five years of his life in one  war shouldn't be called back and have  his family and his life disrupted again  so we're going before Congress on  january the 27th to testify and to urge  upon them the passage of universal  military training realistically  commander do you have much hope that  this Congress will pass it no I think  that there's great pressure upon  Congress to pass a bill yes because  there's a tremendous manpower need in  our armed forces the last time I  discussed this question with the head of  our armed forces he told me that their  biggest problem was manpower and  certainly we need to set up machinery to  get that manpower there's a section  minutes though isn't it on the ground  that this won't work along with the  draft and that you must wait until the  draft is no longer needed you agree to  that well I suppose we'll have to keep  selective service until we get a  universal military training bill but so  many inequities have arisen under it  that certainly they'll have to bring the  legislation up to date miss mahalia  understand you're a mess Association  favors the non deferment of college  students from selective service but  we're also told now that Russia is  training more engineers and scientists  than we are now would you favor taking  these people out of college and taking  them out of the technical services I  agree that we need a train core of  scientists because warfare is now in a  highly scientific state but I also think  it's inequitable to distinguish between  the men who can afford to go to college  and the man who cannot I suppose some  exceptions in universal military  training will have to be made to see  that we get a proper core of scientists  but I don't like to think that a poor  boy has to go to war  and a boy who can afford college doesn't  have to go you think the deferment of  technically trained people is  undemocratic well I think there's a  point there I may ask you this how about  these young fellows from Korea are they  joining up in veterans organization one  here that they're none joiners well I  don't think that's so I feel that for  instance we made a terrific fight for  the benefits under the GI Bill of Rights  so that they would be given to the  Korean veteran and I think they sense  that they need the veterans  organizations in order to forward their  point of view the fight that we made for  the GI Bill of Rights for Korean  veterans certainly has set us out as an  organization that will be supported by  the Korean veteran and I think they are  joining yesterday that there are a lot  of complaints about Korean veterans  they're not getting the same break you  fellas got on the GI Bill of Rights on  home loans and mortgages and things like  that I don't think that so I think that  essentially and substantially they are  getting the same thing that we are of  course the interest rate for housing for  mortgages for guaranteed mortgages for  tis his hire but then interest rates are  higher all around and of course while we  fought against raising that interest  rate we couldn't we couldn't stand the  pressure and we had to give him because  interest rates had risen universally  throughout the company mr. Mahaney why  several veterans organizations you speak  of yours particularly naturally there  are several now why should there be well  it's this way there are 11 million  veterans of World War two are better and  they're all of one age group essentially  they have something in common other  veterans organizations more or less  arose from the earlier age groups  ambit's consist of one age group from  one war we are chartered exclusively by  Congress for the veterans of World War  two see well mr. Mahim ask you is the  final question what do you veterans  really want how do you want to be  treated from by the rest of the United  States well we don't feel it were any  exception the only thing that we want to  do is to advance the cause of the poor  disabled veteran  widow and his orphan there are many  veterans who are so disabled that they  cannot look after themselves we want to  do that job we want to look after the  mothers of these boys who have been left  behind we want to look after their wives  we feel as a job to do we want to do  that job we want to be able to come in  on legislation affecting these veterans  and their families but we asked no no  different treatment from any other  citizen thank you very much commander  hey it's been a great pleasure to have  you here tonight thank you very much the  opinions that you've heard our speakers  express tonight have been entirely their  own the editorial board for this edition  of the lawn gene Cronus Cove was Larry  lesser and Kenneth Crawford our  distinguished guest was Henry J majed a  national commander of the American  veterans of World War two
 

